00:16:00.373 --> 00:16:02.435
<v Daniel>exactly where the next big breakthrough is hiding.</v>

169
00:16:03.035 --> 00:16:03.656
<v Maya>You really can't.</v>

170
00:16:04.457 --> 00:16:12.883
<v Daniel>And that absolute uncertainty is the exact reason we have to keep multiple diverse directions alive right now, even if they don't look as shiny as an LLM.</v>

171
00:16:13.444 --> 00:16:15.265
<v Maya>We have to protect the weird ideas.</v>

172
00:16:16.546 --> 00:16:31.377
<v Daniel>So we'll leave you with this final provocative thought. If our greatest technological breakthroughs are literally designed to consume the very diversity that created them, what abandoned, forgotten app from five years ago actually holds the key to the next epic of</v>

173
00:16:31.417 --> 00:16:31.797
<v Daniel>computing?</v>

174
00:16:32.477 --> 00:16:33.298
<v Maya>It's out there somewhere.</v>

175
00:16:33.847 --> 00:16:36.734
<v Daniel>It is. Just waiting for someone to finally look away from the crowd.</v>
